  tier four  this is a stronger strain of the virus  in the sense it's you know it's more  transmittable  um it's a bouncy virus so obviously  people can catch it  in a you know much much more easier way  we have to do everything possible  like you know i've i've sat here before  all my colleagues have as well  emphasizing the need to keep each other  safe we want to squash this virus  we want to do much more to prevent the  spread of the virus which is why we have  so many measures such as social  distancing  testing but obviously you know just  making sure that the public are aware of  the dangers of this virus  i i suppose the question is though are  the government actually doing everything  they can do at the moment so patreon  valence said that this variant is  everywhere we know that there is an  outbreak in cumbria that is of some  concern  but the words that he used the starkest  statement of where we are right now in  our battle against the coronavirus was  given not by the prime minister  yesterday but by sir patrick  measures are going to need to be  increased in places  we're not going to be seeing them  decrease are we looking at everywhere  being in  tier 4 before too long well i think it's  important to say  you know as this virus changes grows  the government takes proactive measures  we've seen that certainly over the last  72 hours over the weekend we have seen  that happen  the fact of the matter is when you look  at various parts of the country  coronavirus is around i remember sitting  here last week  talking about the surge that we've seen  in the virus look at wales look at the  east of england my own county as well  look at london and the rates of  transmission it is inevitable  as people travel and of course we're  urging people not to travel for the sake  of everybody's health  that you know we have to take strong  measures and we're doing that we're  constantly reviewing these measures as  well  is it inevitable though that we will see  other parts of the country move to tier  four that was the very strong suggestion  given by the chief scientifically well  of course if the virus continues to  spread  then we will take stronger measures  because at the end of the day our  objective is to save lives and to keep  people safe  but right now it's not for me to preempt  any change because obviously there'll be  a natural review mechanism  in two weeks time i i suppose the  problem is that there are there are  figures circulating this morning they  were reported in the times they've been  confirmed as as accurate by our own  deputy political editor  that by february the reasonable worst  case scenario is i mean it's it's pretty  alarming  700 thousand cases a week perhaps by  february  twenty thousand hospital admissions five  thousand deaths i mean first do you  recognize those figures  well i think first of all what i would  say we are in this health pandemic  and our response has been incredibly  strong look at the measures that the  government has undertaken  all based on saving lives and protecting  our nhs as well  we are very conscious of the fact this  is we're into into the winter period now  we're seeing hospitals already you know  experience an increase in demand  so it's right that we take protective  and protect um preventative measures  which this government is constantly  doing  so you know we can't just sort of work  on you know what will happen in february  we have to stop  you know numbers from rising now and we  will take all the necessary steps and  measures required to do so  well then one has to ask given the fact  that the evidence emerging from nerve  tag is that perhaps  children are as susceptible to this  variant  as adults you know the reason the only  reason that the schools were able to  open up after the first lockdown was  scientific evidence suggesting  that transmissibility amongst younger  people was lower  that doesn't that no longer appears to  be the case that does rather suggest  we really need to immediately review  whether or not schools can safely reopen  in general  i think first of all neil it's important  to talk about you know issues and  recognize mass testing for example  you know we've seen in parts of london i  saw this last week and i've been  speaking to  you know various people across public  health england local governments in  london  but also the police in london as well  you know surge in that capacity for mass  testing  we want to protect everyone and of  course we want to protect children too  now recognizing this sort of new strain  of coronavirus  how bouncy it is it's moving around much  more if we have to take measures to  increase testing in schools clearly the  government will do all that  these are preventative and protective  measures but quite frankly you can't  look at them in isolation of course  that's part of the wider approach that  the government is undertaking but i  think it is  true but i think it is also fair to say  home secretary that this morning  you would not be in a position to  guarantee that the schools will reopen  on the first day of term well we want to  keep schools open let me be clear about  that  you do but you can't guarantee that we  will take all the appropriate measures  around protecting children  the health of children and also  protecting teachers and the rest of the  population as well around schools  but i do want to emphasize the role that  mass testing plays  that math testing is up and running  across the country as we know  and we have been obviously speaking  about math testing in schools  and that is something that is under  discussion right now across government  for january when the schools  eventually go back given that the  scientists for the time being at least  seem to be in the dark as to as to why  this variant of the viruses has  increased transmissibility  isn't it possible that we might actually  have to go all the way back  to looking at the social 